Physical Transformation, Labor Demand, and Immigration in the United States: A First-Principles Framework for Studying Body Composition, Work Capacity, Employer Demand, and Immigration, 1945–2026

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

Research Monograph — Final Theoretical Version

---

Abstract

This research develops a first-principles framework for investigating whether the long-term physical transformation of the United States population has interacted with changes in labor demand, occupational structure, automation, and immigration since the Second World War.

The central hypothesis is not that immigrants are inherently more productive, more obedient, or physically superior to native-born Americans. Such claims would be neither scientifically justified nor operationally useful. Instead, the study asks whether measurable changes in human body composition, physical capacity, occupational physical demands, labor-force participation, wages, automation, and immigration have formed a coupled historical system.

The starting point is physical. Human physical work requires metabolic energy, muscular force, oxygen transport, thermoregulation, recovery, and mechanical movement. An occupation therefore imposes a measurable physical workload. Changes in the physical characteristics of a population could, in principle, modify the effective supply of labor for occupations with high physical demands.

At the same time, employers do not respond only to human physiology. They can substitute between workers, capital, technology, organizational design, wages, hours, and recruitment pools. Immigration represents another potential adjustment mechanism.

The research therefore proposes a hierarchical model:

\text{Physical State} \rightarrow \text{Work Capacity} \rightarrow \text{Effective Labor Supply} \rightarrow \text{Employer Cost} \rightarrow \text{Labor Demand} \rightarrow \text{Occupational Composition}.

Immigration is then introduced as one possible component of the adjustment process.

Historical evidence establishes that adult obesity increased substantially in the United States. Among adults aged 20–74, age-adjusted obesity prevalence increased from approximately 13.4% in 1960–1962 to 35.3% in 2011–2012, while extreme obesity increased from approximately 0.9% to 6.6%. More recent NHANES data (August 2021–August 2023) place age-adjusted overall adult obesity near 40.3% and age-adjusted severe obesity near 9.7%, suggesting a possible plateau at elevated levels.

At the same time, foreign-born workers are disproportionately represented in several occupational categories involving physical or manual work. In 2024, foreign-born workers were more likely than native-born workers to work in natural resources, construction and maintenance occupations (13.9% vs. 7.7%), as well as production, transportation and material-moving occupations (15.5% vs. 11.6%).

However, these two observations do not establish causality.

Research on immigrant health provides an important counterpoint: immigrants often arrive with lower average BMI than U.S.-born populations, but BMI and obesity risk tend to increase with duration of residence in the United States. This produces a dynamic of selection, migration, occupational sorting, U.S. exposure, and partial physical convergence.

The proposed study therefore tests competing explanations rather than assuming a predetermined conclusion. The central empirical test is whether the interaction between population physical capacity and occupational physical demand has independent explanatory power for immigrant occupational concentration after controlling for wages, education, automation, demographics, migration networks, institutional factors, and cognitive skills.

---

1. Research Question

The central research question is:

Did the long-term physical transformation of the U.S. population contribute measurably to changes in the composition of the American labor force and to employer demand for immigrant labor between 1945 and 2026?

This question can be decomposed into five subquestions.

Question 1 — Physical transformation

How did the physical characteristics of the U.S. population change after 1945?

Question 2 — Occupational requirements

How did the physical demands of American occupations change during the same period?

Question 3 — Effective labor capacity

Did changes in body composition, physical activity, strength, endurance, disability, and age alter the effective supply of workers for physically demanding occupations?

Question 4 — Employer response

When the effective domestic supply of physically capable workers changed, did employers respond through higher wages, technological substitution, organizational changes, longer or shorter working hours, recruitment from other regions, or immigration?

Question 5 — Immigration

Did immigration disproportionately increase in occupations where the physical requirements of work were high and where domestic labor supply was relatively constrained?

---

2. First-Principles Foundation

2.1 Human work begins with energy

A human worker is a biological energy-conversion system.

The basic energy balance can be represented as:

E_{\text{metabolic}} = W_{\text{external}} + Q_{\text{heat}} + E_{\text{internal}}

where $E_{\text{metabolic}}$ is metabolic energy expenditure, $W_{\text{external}}$ is useful mechanical work, $Q_{\text{heat}}$ is heat dissipation, and $E_{\text{internal}}$ represents other physiological expenditures.

For a simple lifting task:

W = mgh.

Thus, moving a mass $m$ through a vertical distance $h$ requires mechanical energy proportional to $mgh$.

But human work cannot be described by mechanical energy alone. The worker must also sustain:

P = \frac{W}{t},

where $P$ is mechanical power. An occupation requiring repeated lifting, carrying, walking, climbing, bending, or pushing therefore creates a physiological demand that can be studied quantitatively.

2.2 Physical capacity is multidimensional

A fundamental methodological rule of this study is:

\boxed{\text{BMI} \neq \text{physical capacity}}

BMI is useful as a population-level epidemiological indicator, but it does not directly measure muscle mass, muscle strength, aerobic capacity, work endurance, coordination, occupational skill, or recovery capacity.

Consequently, the study must avoid the simplistic proposition that higher BMI necessarily implies lower productivity. The more scientifically defensible model is:

C_{\text{physical}} = f\left( M_{\text{lean}}, F_{\text{muscle}}, VO_{2\max}, M_{\text{fat}}, A, R, D \right)

where $M_{\text{lean}}$ is lean body mass, $F_{\text{muscle}}$ is muscular strength, $VO_{2\max}$ is maximal aerobic capacity, $M_{\text{fat}}$ is fat mass, $A$ is age, $R$ is recovery capacity, and $D$ is disability or physical limitation. This distinction is essential.

2.3 Physical capacity for work is task-specific

Physical capacity is not a single scalar. A worker may have high fat mass but sufficient strength, endurance, skill, and recovery for a given task. Obesity may matter more for some tasks—climbing, prolonged standing, heat stress—than for others.

The Physical Labor Demand Index (PLDI) should therefore be task-specific, capturing distinct dimensions of physical demand rather than collapsing them into a single measure prematurely.

---

3. The Historical Physical Transition

The U.S. population underwent a substantial change in body composition during the second half of the twentieth century.

CDC/NCHS historical data show that age-adjusted obesity among adults aged 20–74 was approximately 13.4% in 1960–1962 and 14.5% in 1971–1974. By 1988–1994 it had reached approximately 23.2%, and by 2011–2012 approximately 35.3%. Age-adjusted extreme obesity increased from approximately 0.9% in 1960–1962 to 6.6% in 2011–2012.

More recent NHANES data (August 2021–August 2023) place age-adjusted overall adult obesity near 40.3% and age-adjusted severe obesity near 9.7%. Crude severe obesity is reported at 9.4%. From 2013–2014 through August 2021–August 2023, age-adjusted obesity did not change significantly, while severe obesity increased from 7.7% to 9.7%.

This represents a large population-level transformation. However, the temporal pattern is important. The dramatic acceleration occurs particularly after the late 1970s and 1980s. Therefore, a first-pass causal model should not simply compare 1945 versus 2026 but should examine the temporal structure: $1945 \rightarrow 1960 \rightarrow 1975 \rightarrow 1985 \rightarrow 1995 \rightarrow 2005 \rightarrow 2015 \rightarrow 2026$.

---

4. The Immigrant Body as a Natural Comparison

One of the most interesting empirical findings for this research is that immigrant populations provide a quasi-natural comparison.

A study using U.S. National Health Interview Survey data found obesity prevalence of approximately 16% among immigrants compared with 22% among U.S.-born individuals in 2000. Among immigrants, age- and sex-adjusted obesity prevalence was approximately 8% for those living in the United States for less than one year, compared with approximately 19% among those living in the country for at least fifteen years.

A systematic review found that a majority of studies reported a significant positive relationship between BMI and duration of U.S. residence.

Another study using objectively measured body composition and physical activity found that immigrants living in the United States for at least fifteen years had lower moderate-to-vigorous physical activity and higher obesity risk than immigrants with shorter residence.

This produces a potentially important dynamic:

\text{Immigrant arrival} \rightarrow \text{initial physical difference} \rightarrow \text{U.S. exposure} \rightarrow \text{partial convergence}.

Therefore, if immigrants were initially recruited partly because of physical characteristics, we might expect their occupational concentration and physical characteristics to change over time. This can be tested.

---

5. Labor as a Physical Production Input

Let total production be:

Y = F(K, L, H),

where $K$ is physical capital, $L$ is quantity of labor, and $H$ is effective human capability.

We can decompose:

H = H_{\text{physical}} + H_{\text{skill}} + H_{\text{health}} + H_{\text{experience}}.

This is not intended as a literal production function yet. It is a conceptual decomposition that can be estimated later. The additive form avoids the unrealistic implication that zero physical capacity implies zero output.

For a physically demanding occupation:

H_{\text{physical}} = f\left( F_{\text{strength}}, VO_{2\max}, \text{mobility}, \text{endurance}, \text{recovery} \right).

The economic question becomes: Does a change in the distribution of $H_{\text{physical}}$ alter the equilibrium price and quantity of labor?

---

6. Employer Optimization

An employer minimizes production cost:

\min_{K, L, I} C_K K + C_L L + C_I I

subject to:

F(K, L, I, H) \geq Y.

Here $K$ is capital/automation, $L$ is domestic labor, and $I$ is immigrant labor.

The important insight is that immigration is not necessarily a substitute for domestic labor. It may be a substitute, a complement, or both, depending on the occupation. Peri and Sparber (2009) found evidence that less-educated immigrants often specialize in manual tasks while native-born workers shift toward interactive, coordinating, organizing, and communicating tasks. Their analysis of U.S. states from 1960–2000 suggests that task specialization can make immigrant and native labor complementary rather than purely substitutive. This is a crucial competing mechanism.

---

7. The Physical-Immigration Hypothesis

We can now define the principal hypothesis more precisely.

H1 — Physical labor-supply hypothesis

If the effective physical capacity of the native-born labor force declines in occupations requiring substantial physical exertion, and if technological substitution is insufficient or costly, employer demand for alternative labor pools should increase.

Formally:

\frac{\partial I}{\partial H_{\text{native, physical}}} < 0

under conditions where immigrant labor is a substitute for domestic physical labor. But this prediction should not be assumed universally. If immigrant workers are complementary to native workers, $\frac{\partial I}{\partial H_{\text{native}}} > 0$ could occur instead. The sign itself therefore becomes an empirical question.

---

8. A More Complete Model

The immigrant labor share in occupation $o$ at time $t$ can be modeled as:

S^I_{o,t} = \alpha + \beta_1 P_{o,t} + \beta_2 W_{o,t} + \beta_3 A_{o,t} + \beta_4 D_{o,t} + \beta_5 M_{o,t} + \beta_6 G_{o,t} + \mu_o + \lambda_t + \epsilon_{o,t},

where $S^I_{o,t}$ is the immigrant share of occupation $o$, $P_{o,t}$ is physical demands, $W_{o,t}$ is wages, $A_{o,t}$ is automation/capital intensity, $D_{o,t}$ is domestic labor supply, $M_{o,t}$ is migration networks, $G_{o,t}$ is geographic factors, $\mu_o$ is occupation fixed effects, and $\lambda_t$ is year fixed effects.

The physical hypothesis predicts $\beta_1 > 0$ if physically demanding occupations disproportionately recruit immigrants after controlling for other factors. But that coefficient alone would still not prove that changing American body composition caused immigration.

---

9. The Stronger Causal Test

The strongest version of the hypothesis requires an interaction:

S^I_{o,r,t} = \alpha + \beta_1 PLDI_{o,t} + \beta_2 PPCI_{r,t} + \beta_3 (PLDI_{o,t} \times PPCI_{r,t}) + X'_{o,r,t}\gamma + \mu_o + \delta_r + \lambda_t + \epsilon_{o,r,t},

where $PLDI_{o,t}$ is the physical intensity of occupation $o$ at time $t$, $PPCI_{r,t}$ is the physical capacity of the population in region $r$ at time $t$, $\mu_o$ is occupation fixed effects, $\delta_r$ is region fixed effects, $\lambda_t$ is year fixed effects, and $X'_{o,r,t}$ is a vector of controls.

The critical coefficient becomes:

\boxed{\beta_3}

If $\beta_3 < 0$ (when $PPCI$ is coded as higher = more capacity) and remains negative after controlling for wages, automation, industrial composition, demographics, geography, education, cognitive skills, and immigration networks, then evidence would exist that lower population physical capacity is associated with disproportionately greater immigrant recruitment into physically demanding occupations. That would be a meaningful result.

To assess the robustness of this finding to unobserved confounding, we employ the Oster (2019) test, which evaluates the magnitude of omitted variable bias by imposing restrictions on the relationship between observed and unobserved selection. Values of Oster's delta $\delta \geq 1$ are interpreted as robustness to omitted variable bias.

---

10. The Alternative Explanations

A rigorous study must attempt to destroy its own hypothesis. At least nine competing explanations must be tested.

Alternative 1 — Wage differentials. Employers may recruit immigrants because immigrants accept lower wages. The average wage elasticity of native labor supply to immigration is approximately −0.2 in the literature, with Borjas finding estimates in the range of −0.3 to −0.4. This quantifies the wage mechanism.

Alternative 2 — Occupational specialization. Immigrants may specialize in manual occupations while native-born workers specialize in communication and coordination. This mechanism is supported by existing economic research (Peri & Sparber, 2009).

Alternative 3 — Demographic aging. The U.S. population has aged. An older workforce can alter occupational labor supply independently of obesity.

Alternative 4 — Automation. Employers may respond to labor scarcity by increasing capital intensity.

Alternative 5 — Globalization. Manufacturing and agricultural production have undergone major structural changes, including trade shocks such as the China shock.

Alternative 6 — Immigration networks. Once immigrant communities become established in a region, information and social networks can reduce the cost of recruiting additional immigrants.

Alternative 7 — Incarceration and criminal justice. The expansion of the criminal justice system removed a substantial number of working-age men from the labor force, particularly in physically demanding occupations.

Alternative 8 — Disability insurance and opioid crisis. Rising disability insurance enrollment and the opioid epidemic reduced labor force participation, especially among less-educated workers.

Alternative 9 — Unionization decline and employer monopsony. The decline of unionization and the fissuring of work arrangements altered wages and working conditions in manual occupations.

Any observed relationship between physical demand and immigration must therefore survive these controls.

Additionally, recent research demonstrates that a significant portion of the immigrant wage penalty is explained by measured cognitive skills (literacy, numeracy) and the downgrading of skills in the host country's labor market. The PIAAC dataset provides direct assessments of adult literacy, numeracy, and adaptive problem-solving skills, and must be incorporated as a critical control. However, PIAAC's historical coverage is limited to recent years. For the long panel, education, occupation, nativity, and duration of residence will necessarily proxy for cognitive and skill differences. This limitation must be stated explicitly and the PIAAC-based analysis treated as a complementary, recent-period validation rather than a continuous historical control.

---

11. Occupational Evidence

Current BLS data already demonstrate that foreign-born workers are not distributed randomly across occupations.

In 2024, 22.0% of foreign-born workers were employed in service occupations versus 15.1% of native-born workers; 13.9% were in natural resources, construction, and maintenance versus 7.7% of native-born workers; and 15.5% were in production, transportation, and material-moving occupations versus 11.6% of native-born workers. Conversely, foreign-born workers were less represented in management, professional and related occupations: 35.4% versus 45.9%.

This pattern is consistent with a physical-task hypothesis. But it is also consistent with education differences, immigration selection, language, credential recognition, occupational networks, wage differences, legal status, and historical settlement patterns.

Therefore:

\text{Observed occupational concentration} \neq \text{proof of physical selection}.

---

12. The Critical Correction to the Original Hypothesis

The original intuitive hypothesis can be stated as: Americans became heavier and less physically capable, therefore employers increasingly substituted immigrants who were thinner and more willing to work.

The evidence currently available does not justify that complete causal chain.

A more defensible hypothesis is:

Changes in population health and physical capacity may have altered the effective supply of labor for some physically demanding occupations, interacting with wages, automation, demographics, migration networks, institutional conditions, and cognitive skill distributions to influence employer demand for immigrant labor.

This is a much stronger scientific proposition because every component can be measured.

---

13. A Potentially Important Paradox

There is an especially interesting empirical paradox. If immigrants are initially physically different from the U.S.-born population, but their body composition becomes more similar to the U.S.-born population with longer residence, then immigrant labor cannot be treated as a permanently distinct biological labor pool.

The process may instead look like:

\text{Selection} \rightarrow \text{Migration} \rightarrow \text{Occupational sorting} \rightarrow \text{U.S. exposure} \rightarrow \text{Physical convergence}.

This could mean that the U.S. labor market is not simply importing a permanently "different" type of worker. It may be importing people whose initial characteristics are partly selected by migration and economic opportunity. Over time, the receiving environment itself changes them. This interpretation is supported by several studies documenting increasing BMI or obesity risk with duration of U.S. residence.

---

14. A Second Paradox: Immigration May Reduce Measured Population Obesity

An especially striking study examined immigration's contribution to the U.S. obesity epidemic and concluded that the U.S. obesity epidemic would have been more severe without the mass immigration beginning in 1965, because newly arrived immigrants tended to have lower BMI than U.S.-born populations, although this advantage eroded with time.

This is extremely important for the theory. It means that immigration and obesity may initially move in opposite directions at the population level:

\text{More immigration} \rightarrow \text{lower aggregate obesity than otherwise}.

But:

\text{Longer immigrant residence} \rightarrow \text{increasing convergence}.

Therefore, the relationship between immigration and physical transformation is likely bidirectional and dynamic rather than a simple one-way causal chain.

