Appendix F: The Immigrant Share Index (ISI) and Occupational Crosswalk Construction

F.1 Purpose

This appendix provides the detailed methodological specifications for constructing the Immigrant Share Index (ISI) and the occupation crosswalks necessary to link health, labor, and immigration data across the 1945–2026 period.

F.2 Definition

The immigrant share in occupation $o$ at time $t$ is defined as:

ISI_{o,t} = \frac{I_{o,t}}{I_{o,t} + N_{o,t}}

where $I_{o,t}$ is the number of foreign-born workers in occupation $o$ at time $t$ and $N_{o,t}$ is the number of native-born workers in occupation $o$ at time $t$.

F.3 Data Sources

· IPUMS Census (1940–2000): Provides harmonized occupation codes and nativity variables for decennial census data.
· ACS (2001–present): Provides annual data with consistent occupation coding.
· CPS (annual): Provides monthly data with occupation and nativity variables.
· Historical immigration records: Provide supplementary information on immigrant flows and characteristics.

F.4 Occupation Coding Systems

Over the study period, several occupation coding systems were used:

Period Coding System
1940–1970 1950 Census occupation codes
1970–1980 1970 Census occupation codes
1980–1990 1980 Census occupation codes
1990–2000 1990 Census occupation codes
2000–2010 2000 SOC codes
2010–present 2010 SOC codes

Crosswalks between these systems are available from IPUMS and the Bureau of Labor Statistics. The crosswalks introduce measurement error, particularly for occupations whose content changed substantially across decades (e.g., due to technological change).

F.5 Linking Occupations to Physical Demand Data

The occupation crosswalk must link historical occupation codes to the PLDI rankings constructed from O*NET and DOT data. This requires:

1. DOT–O*NET crosswalk. The DOT provides physical demand ratings for the period before ONET. The crosswalk between DOT and ONET codes enables the extension of physical demand ratings backward in time.
2. SOC–Census crosswalk. The crosswalk between SOC codes (used in O*NET and ORS) and Census occupation codes (used in IPUMS) enables the linking of physical demand data to historical labor force data.
3. Consistency checks. Where occupations have changed substantially over time, the fixed-ranking approach may introduce measurement error. Sensitivity analyses should test whether results are robust to excluding occupations with major content changes.

F.6 Legal Status Proxy

The CPS and ACS do not directly measure legal status. Several proxy approaches are available:

1. Country-of-birth and year-of-entry combinations. Certain country–year combinations are strongly associated with legal status categories.
2. Occupation-industry profiles. Certain occupation–industry combinations are disproportionately associated with unauthorized employment.
3. Imputation methods. Statistical imputation based on known distributions of legal status by country of origin and year of entry.

Legal status and selection into legality remain partially unobservable and will remain a limitation of the study. All results should be interpreted with this limitation in mind.

