Appendix G: Identification Strategy — Mathematical Specifications and Assumptions

G.1 Purpose

This appendix provides the mathematical specifications and identifying assumptions for the identification strategies outlined in Section 19 of the main monograph.

G.2 Baseline Occupation-Level Panel Model

The baseline model is:

S^I_{o,t} = \alpha + \beta_1 P_o + \beta_2 B_t + \beta_3 (P_o \times B_t) + X'_{o,t}\gamma + \mu_o + \lambda_t + \epsilon_{o,t}

where:

· $S^I_{o,t}$ is the immigrant share of occupation $o$ at time $t$.
· $P_o$ is the time-invariant physical demand of occupation $o$ (PLDI).
· $B_t$ is a time-varying measure of population physical capacity (PPCI).
· $P_o \times B_t$ is the interaction of interest.
· $X'_{o,t}$ is a vector of controls (wages, education, automation, demographics, migration networks, cognitive skills).
· $\mu_o$ is occupation fixed effects.
· $\lambda_t$ is year fixed effects.
· $\epsilon_{o,t}$ is the error term.

The coefficient of interest is $\beta_3$. If $\beta_3 < 0$ (when PPCI is coded as higher = more capacity), this indicates that lower population physical capacity is associated with disproportionately greater immigrant recruitment into physically demanding occupations.

G.3 Progressive Control Addition

The model should be estimated with progressive addition of controls:

Specification Controls Added
(1) Occupation and year fixed effects only
(2) + Wages
(3) + Education composition
(4) + Age structure
(5) + Automation/capital intensity
(6) + Migration networks
(7) + Cognitive skills (PIAAC subsample)
(8) + Industry composition

The stability of $\beta_3$ across specifications provides evidence on the robustness of the physical mechanism to omitted variable bias.

G.4 Oster (2019) Sensitivity Analysis

The Oster (2019) test evaluates the magnitude of omitted variable bias by imposing restrictions on the relationship between observed and unobserved selection. The test statistic $\delta$ represents the degree of selection on unobservables relative to observables that would be needed to drive the estimated coefficient to zero. Values of $\delta \geq 1$ are interpreted as robustness to omitted variable bias, meaning that the unobservables would need to be at least as important as the observables to explain away the estimated effect.

The implementation requires:

1. Estimating the model with and without controls to obtain $R^2$ values.
2. Specifying a maximum $R^2$ value ($R_{\max}$).
3. Computing $\delta$ using the formula provided in Oster (2019).

G.5 Shift-Share (Bartik) Instrument

The shift-share instrument is formed by interacting local industry shares with national industry growth rates. The instrument assumes a pooled exposure research design, where the shares measure differential exposure to common shocks, and identification is based on exogeneity of the shares.

Relevance condition. Historical settlement patterns are strong predictors of current immigrant inflows. The first stage should be reported to document instrument strength.

Exclusion restriction. The exogeneity of the instrument and the validity of the exclusion restriction must be addressed explicitly. Recent methodological work emphasizes that one of the two components—either the "shifts" or the "shares"—must be exogenous. We must clearly state which component is assumed exogenous and defend that assumption.

Defense of exogeneity. The shares component (historical settlement patterns) is plausibly exogenous to current labor market shocks, as these patterns were established decades ago for reasons unrelated to contemporary physical demand. The shifts component (national immigrant inflows) is more difficult to defend as exogenous, as national inflows may respond to aggregate U.S. labor market conditions.

G.6 Difference-in-Differences

The difference-in-differences strategy compares occupations with high versus low physical requirements before and after major immigration or labor-market shocks. Potential event dates include:

1. 1965 Hart-Celler Act. Abolished national-origin quotas and shifted immigration toward Asia and Latin America.
2. 1986 IRCA. Provided amnesty to approximately 2.7 million unauthorized immigrants and imposed employer sanctions.
3. 2008 Great Recession. Reduced labor demand across many manual occupations.

The parallel trends assumption should be tested using pre-treatment data. The treatment effect is estimated as:

\hat{\tau} = \left( \bar{Y}_{\text{high, post}} - \bar{Y}_{\text{high, pre}} \right) - \left( \bar{Y}_{\text{low, post}} - \bar{Y}_{\text{low, pre}} \right)

where "high" and "low" refer to PLDI classification and "pre" and "post" refer to the period before and after the shock.

