Appendix I: Falsification Tests — Formal Specifications

I.1 Purpose

This appendix provides formal specifications for the falsification tests outlined in Section 20 of the main monograph.

I.2 Test 1: Immigration Rises Equally in Low-Physical-Demand Occupations

Null hypothesis: $\beta_1 = 0$ (physical demand does not predict immigrant concentration).

Test: Estimate the baseline model and test whether $\beta_1$ is statistically significantly different from zero.

Interpretation: If $\beta_1 \approx 0$, immigration rises equally in low-physical-demand occupations, and the physical hypothesis has little explanatory power.

I.3 Test 2: Changes in Native Physical Capacity Have No Relationship with Immigrant Occupational Concentration

Null hypothesis: $\beta_3 = 0$ (the interaction between physical demand and population physical capacity does not predict immigrant concentration).

Test: Estimate the interaction model and test whether $\beta_3$ is statistically significantly different from zero.

Interpretation: If $\beta_3 \approx 0$, changes in native physical capacity have no relationship with immigrant occupational concentration.

I.4 Test 3: Wage Differences Explain the Entire Relationship

Null hypothesis: The coefficient on physical demand becomes statistically insignificant when wage controls are added.

Test: Estimate the model with and without wage controls. Compare $\beta_1$ and $\beta_3$ across specifications.

Interpretation: If $\beta_1$ and $\beta_3$ become statistically insignificant when wages are controlled, the wage mechanism dominates.

I.5 Test 4: Automation Explains the Entire Relationship

Null hypothesis: The coefficient on physical demand becomes statistically insignificant when automation controls are added.

Test: Estimate the model with and without automation/capital intensity controls. Compare $\beta_1$ and $\beta_3$ across specifications.

Interpretation: If $\beta_1$ and $\beta_3$ become statistically insignificant when automation is controlled, technological substitution dominates.

I.6 Test 5: Immigration Follows Migration Networks Independently of Physical Demand

Null hypothesis: The coefficient on physical demand becomes statistically insignificant when migration network controls are added.

Test: Estimate the model with and without migration network controls. Compare $\beta_1$ and $\beta_3$ across specifications.

Interpretation: If $\beta_1$ and $\beta_3$ become statistically insignificant when networks are controlled, network effects dominate.

I.7 Test 6: Immigrant Occupational Concentration Is Unchanged After Controlling for Education, Age, Wages, Cognitive Skills, and Industry

Null hypothesis: The physical demand coefficient remains statistically significant after the full set of controls is added.

Test: Estimate the full model with all controls and test whether $\beta_1$ and $\beta_3$ remain statistically significant.

Interpretation: If the physical demand coefficients become statistically insignificant with the full controls, the physical hypothesis is rejected.

I.8 Test 7: The Relationship Disappears When Physical-Demand Measures Are Improved

Null hypothesis: The physical demand coefficient remains statistically significant when improved physical-demand measures are used.

Test: Re-estimate the model using alternative PLDI specifications (e.g., ORS-based, DOT-based, task-specific subindices) and compare results.

Interpretation: If the relationship disappears when physical-demand measures are improved, the original result was an artifact of measurement error.

