Appendix J: What Would Count as Strong Evidence — Formal Specification

J.1 The Causal Chain

The strongest possible evidence would be a chain such as:

\boxed{\text{Physical transformation} \rightarrow \text{reduced effective labor supply} \rightarrow \text{higher labor cost} \rightarrow \text{employer adjustment} \rightarrow \text{increased immigrant recruitment}}

J.2 Temporal Precedence

The chain requires temporal precedence:

1. $\Delta \text{PhysicalCapacity}_t < 0$ (population physical capacity declines).
2. Followed by $\Delta \text{Wage}_{\text{physical}, t} > 0$ (wages rise in physically demanding occupations).
3. Followed by $\Delta \text{ImmigrantShare}_{\text{physical}, t} > 0$ (immigrant share rises in physically demanding occupations).

J.3 Robustness Requirements

The chain must hold:

· After controlling for automation, education, age, industry, migration networks, cognitive skills, and region.
· Across multiple specifications (progressive control addition).
· With Oster $\delta \geq 1$ for omitted variable bias.
· Across multiple PLDI specifications.
· Across multiple PPCI specifications.

J.4 The Null Results

If $\beta_{\text{physical}} \approx 0$ after controlling for wages, automation, and education, the physical hypothesis would have little explanatory power. If $\beta_{\text{wage}} \gg \beta_{\text{physical}}$, the principal mechanism would likely be economic rather than physiological. If $\beta_{\text{automation}} \gg \beta_{\text{physical}}$, technological substitution would dominate.

This is precisely why a first-principles framework is useful. It does not begin with the conclusion.

