Appendix L: Preliminary Findings — Statistical Documentation

L.1 Proposition 1: Physical Composition Changed Dramatically

The evidence for this proposition is documented in Appendix A. The key statistics are:

· Obesity prevalence increased from 13.4% (1960–1962) to 40.3% (August 2021–August 2023) among adults ages 20–74.
· Severe obesity increased from 0.9% (1960–1962) to 9.7% (August 2021–August 2023).

L.2 Proposition 2: Foreign-Born Workers Are Disproportionately Represented in Manual Occupations

The evidence for this proposition is documented in Appendix C. The key statistics are:

· Foreign-born workers are overrepresented in natural resources, construction, and maintenance (13.9% vs. 7.7% native-born).
· Foreign-born workers are overrepresented in production, transportation, and material moving (15.5% vs. 11.6% native-born).
· Foreign-born workers are underrepresented in management, professional, and related occupations (35.4% vs. 45.9% native-born).

L.3 Proposition 3: Immigrant BMI Advantage Diminishes with Duration of Residence

The evidence for this proposition is documented in Appendix B. The key statistics are:

· Obesity prevalence among immigrants: 8% for <1 year residence, 19% for ≥15 years residence.
· After adjusting for confounders, living in the U.S. for ≥15 years was associated with a BMI increase of 1.39 BMI points.

L.4 Proposition 4: Task Specialization and Complementarity

The evidence for this proposition is documented in Appendix H.3. Peri and Sparber (2009) demonstrated that foreign-born workers specialize in occupations intensive in manual-physical labor skills while native-born workers pursue jobs more intensive in communication-language tasks. This mechanism can explain why immigrant and native labor may be complementary rather than purely substitutive.

