Appendix O: Scientific Status — Final Assessment

O.1 Established Findings

1. Substantial increase in U.S. obesity. Obesity prevalence increased from 13.4% (1960–1962) to 40.3% (August 2021–August 2023) among adults ages 20–74. Severe obesity increased from 0.9% to 9.7% over the same period.
2. Occupational concentration of foreign-born workers. Foreign-born workers are disproportionately represented in manual and physical occupations, including construction and maintenance (13.9% vs. 7.7%) and production, transportation, and material moving (15.5% vs. 11.6%).
3. Immigrant BMI/obesity differences associated with duration of U.S. residence. Immigrants arrive with lower average BMI than U.S.-born populations, but this difference diminishes with longer residence. The prevalence of obesity was 8% among immigrants living in the United States for less than one year, compared with 19% among those living in the United States for at least fifteen years.
4. Task specialization between immigrant and native-born workers. Peri and Sparber (2009) demonstrated that foreign-born workers specialize in manual-physical labor skills while native-born workers specialize in communication-language tasks, making immigrant and native labor complementary rather than purely substitutive.
5. Declining unionization and rising monopsony in manual occupations. Union membership declined from approximately 35% of the workforce in the 1950s to approximately 10% by the 2020s.
6. Expansion of disability insurance and opioid-related labor force withdrawal. Rising disability insurance enrollment and the opioid epidemic reduced labor force participation, especially among less-educated workers.

O.2 Not Established

1. That U.S. obesity caused employer substitution toward immigrant labor. The available evidence does not justify the complete causal chain from population physical transformation to employer demand for immigrant labor. The correlation between obesity trends and immigrant occupational concentration is consistent with multiple explanations.

O.3 Required Next Step

1. Construct the historical database. Assemble health, labor, and immigration data for the 1945–2026 period.
2. Build the occupational crosswalk. Link historical occupation codes to PLDI rankings.
3. Estimate the occupation-level models. Use the interaction specification with progressive control addition.
4. Apply the Oster (2019) sensitivity analysis. Assess robustness to omitted variable bias.
5. Pre-register the analysis. Specify the primary outcome, interaction, controls, and falsification tests before final data collection.

O.4 Core Scientific Question

\boxed{\text{Whether population-level physical transformation has independent explanatory power for the evolution of immigrant labor demand after controlling for economic, technological, demographic, cognitive-skill, and institutional variables.}}

O.5 Methodological Rule

\boxed{\text{Observation} \rightarrow \text{Measurement} \rightarrow \text{Model} \rightarrow \text{Prediction} \rightarrow \text{Falsification} \rightarrow \text{Conclusion}}

Not:

\text{Ideology} \rightarrow \text{Conclusion} \rightarrow \text{Selective evidence}

The objective is to discover what actually happened.

